\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Momentum blocks under inversion and conjugation}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On a finite periodic grid take fields with values in
#{S\otimes F}, where #{S} is a complex Hermitian carrier of
self-adjoint Euclidean direction operators #{\varepsilon_j} and
#{F} is a nonzero complex Hermitian fiber. Use ordinary
translations and denote the direction operators tensored with
#{\bbI_F} by the same letters. Choose an axis #{t}. Let #{h}
fix coordinate #{t} and negate the others. Let #{\theta_s}
negate coordinate #{t} and fix the others, the coordinate
formula for the \vocabk{site reflection}{fdip-007M}. For a
coordinate reversal #{b}, put #{(b^*f)(y)=f(by)}. Then the
unitary #{\mathcal P=\varepsilon_t h^*} of \ref{fdip-013T}
takes block #{r} onto block #{hr}.}

\p{If a site-independent antiunitary #{\mathcal Q} acts on
values with #{\mathcal Q\varepsilon_j\mathcal Q^{-1}
=\eta\varepsilon_j} for one sign #{\eta} and all #{j}, its
sitewise action takes #{r} to #{-r}. Suppose an antiunitary
time map #{S_t} acts by #{\theta_s^*} followed by a fixed
antiunitary on values. It takes #{r}
to #{-\theta_s r}. For distinct axes #{a,b} spatial relative
to #{t}, of equal period, #{\mathcal P} and #{\mathcal Q}
preserve the transverse turn
#{u_{ab}=(\bbI+\varepsilon_a\varepsilon_b)/\sqrt{2}}
under conjugation. So does #{S_t} if its value action fixes
#{\varepsilon_a\varepsilon_b} by conjugation.}

\proof{
\p{The fields in block #{r} have the form
#{y\mapsto\alpha_r(y)v} by \ref{fdip-016D}. The coordinate
reversals are their own inverses, so #{\alpha_r(by)=\alpha_{br}(y)}.
A site-independent antiunitary conjugates the character to
#{\alpha_{-r}}. This gives all three labels and, since all value
maps are bijective, maps onto the indicated blocks.}

\p{The spatial directions anticommute with #{\varepsilon_t}.
Conjugation by #{\varepsilon_t} supplies two minus signs on
their product. For #{\mathcal Q} the two factors supply
#{\eta^2=1}. The coefficients of #{u_{ab}} are real. Under
the stated condition the time map's value action fixes the same turn. On fields
the turn with coordinate image #{g} acts by
#{f\mapsto(y\mapsto u_{ab}f(g^{-1}y))}, as in \ref{fdip-016Y}.
That rotation commutes with #{h} and #{\theta_s}
because the two axes are spatial, and its pullback commutes
with all site-independent value maps. These conjugations
therefore preserve the full lifted turn.}

\p{For example, given a commuting-sign sitewise #{\mathcal Q_+}
and a site-independent self-adjoint grading #{\Gamma} anticommuting with all
directions, one such value action is
#{\Gamma\varepsilon_t\mathcal Q_+^{-1}}. It is antiunitary.
Each spatial generator commutes with both #{\mathcal Q_+^{-1}}
and #{\Gamma\varepsilon_t}, giving the transverse identity.
The maps #{c_\pm\otimes c_F} in \ref{fdip-013T} supply
#{\mathcal Q} on its Pauli carrier with a fiber antiunitary.}
}
